#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ba98cd4-d69c-5495-af9b-72830b9e89cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292354} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4c72-2fc5-5d15-a061-2e738b5c790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292355} {:answer/id #uuid "91da35ad-83ab-5130-a473-bb967c133713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292356} {:answer/id #uuid "b18890cf-1605-52fe-8586-d087db666e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292357} {:answer/id #uuid "72971519-5585-5650-8c38-d018bd7e9b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba98cd4-d69c-5495-af9b-72830b9e89cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292354} :answer-field/id #uuid "04039f26-3365-5f29-bdb0-7f8b8b5a33c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1e4171a-6ffc-51f5-860a-01422535957d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85077" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a7f402-aecc-5624-9d20-8866c52765a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292368} {:answer/id #uuid "e49af0fc-933e-5e31-ace0-05d5066b6079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292369} {:answer/id #uuid "12cf6aa2-2da2-5697-b58b-18c03200ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292370} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8971b1-0e3a-5c9f-b154-e0e170c86f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292371} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa3c7ed-ee51-52e5-8e20-281743c9ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aa3c7ed-ee51-52e5-8e20-281743c9ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292372} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d05ff4-b74f-5e7e-a6fe-cb615b2a22f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d31fcdd1-6a95-5433-a4d6-af70cdf371e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-9263" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
